 standard deviation below and 1 standard deviation above its mean?","position":{"start":{"line":36,"column":1},"end":{"line":36,"column":1}},"key":"qAlE3VmO8n"}],"key":"Bn2k2mE50i"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":38,"column":1},"end":{"line":38,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"To find probabilities between two values, we must put them in terms of “less than” probabilities. A sketch is especially helpful here:","position":{"start":{"line":38,"column":1},"end":{"line":38,"column":1}},"key":"K0BsTMqywJ"}],"key":"WfI5LGj67x"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":40,"column":1},"end":{"line":40,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"P(−1 < Z < +1) = P(Z < +1) − P(Z < −1) = 0.8413 − 0.1587 = 0.6826.","position":{"start":{"line":40,"column":1},"end":{"line":40,"column":1}},"key":"oY7x4xTpmA"}],"key":"mBoA69qjiG"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":42,"column":1},"end":{"line":42,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"(Note that this confirms the “68%” of the Standard Deviation Rule, now with more precision.)","position":{"start":{"line":42,"column":1},"end":{"line":42,"column":1}},"key":"uLJAoL676g"}],"key":"IHeIT9uuXM"}],"class":"tip","key":"JmN4rqsuvS"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":45,"column":1},"end":{"line":45,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Check Your Understanding: Greater-Than and Between Probabilities","position":{"start":{"line":45,"column":1},"end":{"line":45,"column":1}},"key":"D6ibhuImj3"}],"identifier":"check-your-understanding-greater-than-and-between-probabilities","label":"Check Your Understanding: Greater-Than and Between Probabilities","html_id":"check-your-understanding-greater-than-and-between-probabilities","implicit":true,"key":"HANLiORw5w"},{"type":"anywidget","esm":"/textbook/build/quiz-widget-fa7b2b23175943b1af7aff3871fe44e5.mjs","model":{"question":"What is P(Z > −1.35)? (From the table, P(Z < −1.35) = 0.0885.)","choices":[{"correct":true,"text":"0.9115","feedback":"Correct! P(Z > −1.35) = 1 − 0.0885 = 0.9115."},{"correct":false,"text":"0.0885","feedback":"0.0885 is the probability of being LESS than −1.35."},{"correct":false,"text":"0.5","feedback":"By symmetry P(Z > −1.35) = P(Z < 1.35), which is far more than half."}],"multi":false,"hint":"\"Greater than\" is the complement of the left-tail probability.","explanation":""},"key":"KUqjqU9DxJ"},{"type":"anywidget","esm":"/textbook/build/quiz-widget-fa7b2b23175943b1af7aff3871fe44e5.mjs","model":{"question":"What is P(−1.47 < Z < 2.8)? (Use the values found in the example: P(Z < 2.8) = 0.9974 and P(Z < −1.47) = 0.0708.)","choices":[{"correct":true,"text":"0.9266","feedback":"Correct! 0.9974 − 0.0708 = 0.9266."},{"correct":false,"text":"1.0682","feedback":"Adding the two left-tail probabilities doesn't correspond to any area between the two values."},{"correct":false,"text":"0.9974","feedback":"0.9974 is the entire area to the left of 2.8; you must remove the part below −1.47."}],"multi":false,"hint":"Subtract the smaller left-tail area from the larger.","explanation":""},"key":"ul8Q1jOCnm"}],"key":"dr4dv4R6kl"}],"key":"Gr0zj76yGS"},"references":{"cite":{"order":[],"data":{}}},"footer":{"navigation":{"prev":{"title":"Reading the Standard Normal Table","url":"/pages/m11-standard-normal-table-1-of-3","group":"Section 8: Random Variables"},"next":{"title":"Working Backward: From Probability to z","url":"/pages/m11-standard-normal-table-3-of-3","group":"Section 8: Random Variables"}}},"domain":"http://localhost:3000"}